{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "Buggy Post-Task Review",
  "description": "After Kiro finishes a coding task, review the Python files it changed: confirm they parse, recall any defects already on record, and flag risky new code. Does not execute the project's code and does not edit anything - investigation is offered, never performed unasked.",
  "version": "1",
  "when": {
    "type": "agentStop"
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "The agent just finished a coding task. Review what changed - do NOT edit any code automatically:\n\n1. Identify which .py files were modified in this session (git diff, or recent file edits).\n2. For each, call buggy_functions to confirm the file still parses and to see which functions are investigable.\n3. Call buggy_recall for the functions that were touched, to surface defects already proven against them. A 'regressed' status means a defect that had been fixed is failing again - say so immediately.\n4. Flag any new code with a risky shape: division without a zero check, indexing without a bounds check, unclamped percentage or money arithmetic, or a function that can return None where the caller assumes a value.\n\nSteps 2 and 3 only read. Do NOT call buggy_investigate, buggy_scan or buggy_repair on your own: those execute the module, which runs import-time side effects. Instead, name the one or two functions most worth proving and ask whether to investigate them.\n\nIf the user agrees, call buggy_repair on the named function. It returns a proven trigger plus candidate patches, each with a verdict of 'verified', 'overfit' or 'rejected' and the evidence behind it - there is no numeric score. Show the user the diff of a verified patch and let them choose; apply it with str_replace only if they say yes, then call buggy_investigate on that function to confirm the fix held.\n\nKeep it concise. If nothing changed and nothing is on record, say nothing."
  }
}
